  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe aim to improve the upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by leveraging smooth optimization on a coarse grid expanded through multi-resolution analysis. The central idea is to:\n- 1. Use a novel initialization inspired by mathematical structures typical of optimal solutions\n- 2. Perform coarse optimization with constrained gradient descent\n- 3. Expand grid through adaptive upsampling\n- 4. Combine multi-start methods with grid expansion\n\nThe current record is 0.3808, and our approach focuses on generating well-behaved initial points using mathematical functions while preserving structure and feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Function represented by n-point samples, dx=2/n\n- Objective: Minimize max_k \u210e(x)(1-\u210e(x+k)) dx \u2192 Minimize max correlation (via np.correlate)\n- Constraints:\n  \u2211h[i]*dx = 1 \u21d2 \u2211h[i] = n/2\n  0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Initialization: Use logarithmic mapping of sine function to achieve structured patterns\n- Algorithm selection:\n  \u2022 Use quadratic programming for constrained optimization\n  \u2022 Apply grid expansion via interpolation\n  \u2022 Use sum-preserving upsampling\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialize with sum-preserving function:\n- Base grid: 190 points (from initial_h_values)\n- Iterative refinement:\n  1. Sample grid uniformly, but use interpolation to create new grid points\n  2. Maintain sum constraint via repair function\n  3. Score candidate using np.correlate-multiplied by dx\n  4. Select candidates by improvement values\n  5. Expand grid exponentially (190, 380, 760) until budget expiration\n\n4. Search choices\n- Base candidates: Use sine-derived base functions with controlled spectral properties\n- Expansion path: Triangular support expansion reducing non-overlap regions (2 samples per strategy)\n- Repair method: Gradient-based repair preserving sum\n- Optimization method: CVXPY-QP formulation ensuring convex subproblem\n\n5. Numerical robustness\n- Grid expansion: Maintain regular interpolation through positional mapping\n- Memory: Store only current best solution\n- Constraint enforcement: Gradient projection repair within feasible set\n- Repair failure: Return to parent solution\n\n6. Runtime and return contract\n- Budget: 1100 seconds\n- Time allocation:\n  \u2022 20% parent grid optimization\n  \u2022 40% grid expansion\n  \u2022 40% local search\n- Return format (h_values, float(C5_bound), int(n_points))",
